READABILITY REVIEW
 
Author of code review: Sean Kong
Date of code review: 2026-11-16
Sprint number: Sprint 4
File reviewed: UI/Hud.cs
Author of file reviewed: Frank An
Time taken to complete review: 10 minutes
 
COMMENTS - WHAT IS READABLE:
- The class is well commented. Almost every constant and non-obvious step has a short
  comment saying why, not just what: the frame is as wide as a room, the hearts fill the
  bottom row first, the sword is 7 pixels wide in an 8 pixel slot so it is shifted half a
  pixel. A new reader can follow the layout without opening the sprite sheet.
- Names are descriptive and consistent: LevelLabelAt, RupeeCounterAt, HeartsAt,
  DrawCount, DrawNumber, DrawHearts, DrawMap. The Draw method reads like a table of
  contents for the HUD (frame, blank the stand-ins, label, counters, items, hearts, map).
- Layout values are named constants (HeartsPerRow, MaxCountShown, GlyphSize, MapRoomWidth)
  instead of bare numbers, and positions are grouped under one "Where things go on the
  frame" comment.
- Each helper is short (under 15 lines) and does one thing. Screen() and Fill() hide the
  scale and origin math so the draw methods only deal in sheet pixels.
- The two Draw overloads are explained (top of the screen vs. anywhere, for the pause
  screen), so it is clear why both exist.
 
COMMENTS - WHAT IS NOT READABLE:
- In DrawMap, the local variable "Point origin" has the same name as the private field
  "origin" (the top-left corner of the HUD on screen). Inside that block "origin" means a
  map cell's position in sheet pixels, but everywhere else it means the HUD's position on
  screen. A reader has to notice the shadowing to avoid misreading it, and Fill() on the
  next line silently uses the field, not the local. A different name such as roomAt would
  fix it.
- The "origin" field is assigned inside Draw and then read by Screen() and Fill(), so a
  reader has to know that those helpers only work after Draw has set it. Nothing in the
  names or comments says so.


CODE QUALITY REVIEW
 
Author of code review: Sean Kong
Date of code review: 2026-11-16
Sprint number: Sprint 4
File reviewed: UI/Hud.cs
Author of file reviewed: Frank An
 
COMMENTS ON CODE QUALITY:
- Strengths: the class has one job (draw the HUD for a given player and room manager) and
  it does not change game state. It depends on the IPlayer interface and RoomManager
  rather than the concrete Link, the layout is data driven (named positions, scale
  applied in one place), and the counters, hearts and map are clamped (MaxCountShown,
  MaxHearts) so the drawing cannot overflow the frame.
- Hidden mutable state in Draw: Draw(spriteBatch, at) writes the "origin" field and the
  helper methods (Screen, Fill) read it. Because of this, the helpers are only correct if
  they are called during a Draw, and the class is not safe to draw twice at different
  positions at the same time (for example, if the pause screen and the gameplay view ever
  draw it in the same frame). Passing the origin as a parameter, or using a small struct
  that holds the origin and scale, would remove the hidden dependency.
- Sprite creation is split between field initializers and the constructor (digits and
  bItems are filled in the constructor, the others at the field). Putting them all in one
  place would make it easier to see what the object owns.

 
HYPOTHETICAL CHANGE:
Suppose a future sprint adds half hearts (damage of half a heart) and a second row of
status, such as a compass or the dungeon map item that only shows the map once Link has
picked it up.
- Half hearts: DrawHearts picks between only two sprites (full and empty) by comparing
  the heart's index with Health, and Health is an int counted in whole hearts. Adding half
  hearts means changing Link's health units (for example to half-heart points), adding a
  half-heart sprite to HudSpriteFactory, and changing the index math in DrawHearts. The
  change would be local to DrawHearts plus the factory, which is a good sign, but it also
  touches Link and everything that calls LoseHealth.
- Map visibility: DrawMap currently draws every room that has a MapPosition and does not
  know which rooms Link has visited or whether he owns the map item. Hud would need to ask
  the player or room manager for that, which means changing IPlayer or RoomManager, then
  every class that implements them, not only Hud.
